[
  {
    "_doc": "Copy to .qa/known-defects.json (drop this _doc key) and edit. THE REGISTRY IS A TOP-LEVEL JSON ARRAY of entries -- one exactly-one JSON document, nothing before or after it -- so there is no room for a file-level _doc the way .qa/config.json.example has one; this key rides inside the example entry instead, where scripts/known-defects.sh ignores it. WHY IT EXISTS: a known-but-unfixed application defect used to be smuggled into a QA checklist as a criterion that EXPECTS the crash, which grades the crash CORRECT. A defect is not a verdict -- an entry here has no pass/fail and contributes nothing to the tally; it only stops one already-known failure from reddening a run. PROJECT-LEVEL, NOT PER-SPEC (R16): a defect is a property of the APPLICATION, not of a QA target, so ONE defect has ONE ticket and ONE expiry in this ONE file. Per-spec copies drift, and whichever copy is most convenient is the one that gets renewed. REQUIRED FIELDS, each a non-blank single-line string: id, title, ticket, expiry, severity, observedClass, surface, observedBehaviour. Optional: observedStatus (a number). observedClass is one of non-rendering | wrong-value | degraded -- a structured enum, because the SEVERITY FLOOR gates on it and not on prose: observedClass \"non-rendering\" REQUIRES severity high or critical. A gate that grepped observedBehaviour would be defeated by rewording; this is the check that rejects the originating incident's severity \"low\" on a page that did not render. severity is one of low | medium | high | critical. EXPIRY IS CAPPED AT 90 DAYS from today (R2). Without a cap, \"2099-01-01\" makes the whole registry a paper rule. Past the date the entry reports as expired, and RENEWAL IS A DELIBERATE ACT: it needs an explicit new date, typed by a human and visible in a diff -- never an automatic extension. An entry with no valid deadline counts as expired by definition. ticket is the named owner: a real issue URL or tracker id. An entry without both an owner and a deadline is \"deferred by design\" under a new name, so both are required. surface is the path (query only when it matters) where the defect is observable; a {placeholder} segment matches one-or-more characters that are not / ? or &, so \"/admin/challenges/{challenge}?tab=gates\" matches https://app.test/admin/challenges/1?tab=gates. CLEARING REQUIRES POSITIVE EVIDENCE (R4). An entry becomes cleared ONLY when the evidence rendered from a run shows a RECORDED 2xx NAVIGATION TO ITS SURFACE and no fatal finding on that surface. ABSENCE OF A FINDING NEVER CLEARS ANYTHING -- a run that never reached the surface produces exactly the same silence as a fixed defect, so silence is reported as \"not exercised this run\", never as fixed. Evidence urls must be ABSOLUTE; a relative or path-less url proves nothing and is ignored on the navigation side and blocks clearing on the finding side. NO STRING FIELD MAY CONTAIN A CONTROL CHARACTER (anything below U+0020, or U+007F) -- registry prose is single-line by contract, and a newline inside a value could otherwise forge a status row. Keep every value on one line; do not pretty-wrap prose into it. Entries are usually written for you: qa-kit/scripts/migrate-inverted-criterion.sh lifts an inverted criterion out of a checklist and appends it here with ticket and expiry left EMPTY and exit 2 (its success path), so validate deliberately fails until a human fills both in. The migration also records a migratedFrom field. VALIDATION IS AUTOMATIC: every /qa-run validates this registry for you -- qa-verify.sh calls known-defects.sh validate itself and FAILS THE RUN on any malformed entry or any entry past its expiry, so a broken registry cannot sit unnoticed. You do NOT need to run anything by hand. If you want to check before committing, note that known-defects.sh lives in the qa-e2e-pilot PLUGIN root and not in your project, so there is no portable command to quote here (a plugin cannot address a sibling plugin root -- ADR-0022); run it from wherever that plugin is installed, or let the run tell you. An earlier draft of this note quoted a bash command as if it were runnable from your project: it is not, and it exits 127 -- exit 0 iff every entry is well-formed, otherwise one \"ERROR: entry[<i>].<field>: ...\" line per violation on stderr. Status: bash scripts/known-defects.sh status .qa/known-defects.json <YYYY-MM-DD> [--evidence <file>] prints outstanding | expired | cleared per entry. Unlike .qa/config.json, this file is NOT gitignored -- keep it in version control, which is what makes a renewal or a removal visible in review.",
    "id": "KD-1",
    "title": "Evaluation criteria do not render on a challenge's Gates tab",
    "ticket": "https://github.com/acme/pioneer-dashboard/issues/4312",
